---
import ContentPage from "@/layouts/ContentPage.astro";
import { APP_NAME } from "@/lib/appConfig";

export const prerender = true;

// The privacy policy for the site and the iOS app, and the URL App Store
// Connect lists. Every claim here is about what the code does today: if a
// change adds a service, a stored field or a new use of location, update this
// page in the same change, and bump UPDATED.
const UPDATED = "November 7, 2026";

const h2 = "font-hero text-hero mt-12 text-[1.6rem] leading-tight tracking-tight";
const p = "text-muted mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed";
const ul = "text-muted mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed";
---

<ContentPage
  title={`Privacy — ${APP_NAME}`}
  description={`What ${APP_NAME} collects, why, and who else sees it.`}
  heading="Privacy"
>
  <p class={p}>Last updated {UPDATED}.</p>

  <p class={p}>
    {APP_NAME} is an independent project by James Mitofsky. This policy covers the {APP_NAME} website
    (waterrun.app) and the {APP_NAME} app for iPhone. In short: there are no ads, no analytics, no tracking,
    and nothing is sold. We only handle what the app needs to find fountains, plan a run and save what
    you report to the map.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>Your location</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    The app uses your location while you're using it, to show where you are and find fountains near
    you. During a run, if you allow it, it also uses your location in the background so it can keep
    guiding you with the screen locked. It doesn't use your location in the background outside a
    run. On the website, location is used only if you tap the locate button and your browser allows
    it.
  </p>
  <p class={p}>
    To search an area or plan a route, the app and the website send our server the map coordinates
    involved: the area to search, or the start and stops to route through. Our server passes them on
    to public OpenStreetMap services to find fountains and to BRouter to draw the route. Those
    services see our server, not you. We don't keep these requests or tie them to you, and your
    recorded runs stay on your phone.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>What you report goes on a public map</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    {APP_NAME} saves what you report (a fountain working, broken or gone, details like a bottle filler
    or dog bowl, a short note, or a new fountain) to OpenStreetMap, the free public map of the world.
    Edits there are public by design: anyone can see what changed, where, when, and the OpenStreetMap
    username that made the change, and the edit stays in the map's history. OpenStreetMap's own <a
      href="https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">privacy policy</a
    > covers your account and your edits there.
  </p>
  <p class={p}>
    Until you sign in, your reports wait on your phone and go nowhere. After an edit is sent, our
    server keeps a copy of the changed fountain for a day, so searches show it before
    OpenStreetMap's search services catch up, and logs a line naming the fountain, the change and
    the OpenStreetMap changeset number. Neither copy says who you are, though the changeset itself
    is public.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>Signing in with OpenStreetMap</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    {APP_NAME} has no accounts of its own. To send reports you sign in with an OpenStreetMap account,
    on OpenStreetMap's own page, so we never see your password. OpenStreetMap gives {APP_NAME} a key that
    lets it read your profile and make map edits for you, and nothing else.
  </p>
  <ul class={ul}>
    <li>
      In the app, that key is kept in your iPhone's Keychain. On the website, it's kept in a secure
      cookie that expires after a week.
    </li>
    <li>Our server passes the key to OpenStreetMap with each edit and doesn't store it.</li>
    <li>
      From your profile, the app shows your username, picture, number of contributions and the date
      you joined.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class={p}>
    Sign out from the Profile tab in the app. To take {APP_NAME}'s access away entirely, revoke it
    in your OpenStreetMap account settings.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>Kept on your device</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Your recorded runs, the route you're planning and any reports not sent yet are stored on your
    phone (or in your browser on the website), so nothing is lost if you're offline. They stay there
    until you delete the app or clear the site's data. Run notifications and the Lock Screen Live
    Activity are created on your phone. We don't send push notifications.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>The waitlist and feedback forms</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    If you join the waitlist, we collect your name and email. If you send feedback, we collect your
    message and, if you choose to give one, your email. Both forms are handled by Formspree, which
    stores them and forwards them to us by email. We use them only to reply to you and to tell
    waitlist members when the app launches. Ask through the feedback form and we'll delete what you
    sent.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>Who else is involved</h2>
  <p class={p}>Using {APP_NAME} means these services handle some of your data:</p>
  <ul class={ul}>
    <li>Vercel hosts the website and our server, and keeps request logs for a short time.</li>
    <li>
      OpenFreeMap serves the website's map tiles, and OpenStreetMap's tile servers serve the app's.
      Your browser or phone loads them directly, so those services see your IP address and the area
      of the map you're viewing. The app also loads the font for its map labels from MapLibre's demo
      server.
    </li>
    <li>OpenStreetMap handles sign-in and stores the edits you make.</li>
    <li>Formspree receives the waitlist and feedback forms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class={h2}>Children</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    {APP_NAME} isn't directed at children under 13, and we don't knowingly collect their personal information.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>Changes and contact</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    If this policy changes, we'll update this page and the date at the top. Questions or requests
    about your data go through the <a href="/feedback">feedback form</a>. Leave an email if you'd
    like a reply.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
